ntent {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157082 :question/id #uuid "726d5902-76f0-5321-bf3b-678fe487c831" :question/math-academy-id "e-7124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5784bae8-d32b-522d-91c1-df798c1ad1d5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417114}] :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Statement by Distributing a Disjunction"} :step/id #uuid "6cd4b57c-f668-5ec7-be21-0bd6afcf4614" :step/math-academy-id 26194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157083 :question/id #uuid "ef6f99c1-d605-5b09-983d-05837b464e07" :question/math-academy-id "e-7125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c12386-8f44-5a50-af97-25cb87ae649f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417118}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using the Distributive Property for Disjunctions"} :step/id #uuid "0df7beb5-c114-51ca-8751-453f137e4362" :step/math-academy-id 26195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158481 :tutorial/content "Just like we can distribute multiplication over addition, we can also distribute conjunctions over disjunctions (and vice versa).\n\n- We can distribute a conjunction over a disjunction, as follows: $$A \\land (B \\lor C) \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C)$$\n- Likewise, we can distribute a disjunction over a conjunction, as follows: $$A \\lor (B \\land C) \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C)$$\n\nThe rules above are called the **distributive laws** for propositional logic. To prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables.\n\n- The following truth table shows that $A \\land (B \\lor C) \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C){:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\land (B \\lor C) \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n\n- The following truth table shows that $A \\lor (B \\land C) \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C){:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\lor (B \\land C) \\,$ | $\\, (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "d5455bd3-2038-5ab8-ac8b-2dd7ec1df636" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4343 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "30df3b4d-7faa-590a-8e72-6045fda318f6" :step/math-academy-id 26412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158099}}] :activity/title "The Distributive Laws" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157479}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417123} :activity/id #uuid "86df087c-1ebe-5eea-99bc-4d1806f8526e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049989 :topic/id #uuid "2448a73d-2f61-51ae-8def-fd990d9c6f94" :topic/math-academy-id 2822} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f4fd49-eb1d-5f7b-bb7c-252ea3c21409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∧(¬Y∨¬Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417084} {:answer/id #uuid "193ee776-7e86-50fd-afd3-265ee6678a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∧(Y∨Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417088} {:answer/id #uuid "28cd9cbe-2972-5e59-a8ce-2b964ba0ca45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∧(Y∨Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417089} {:answer/id #uuid "514c9ad6-ef2e-509d-8c54-77e28b6bcd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∨(Y∧Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417099} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf94273-9cd0-5a33-bb6c-bfe5aac65062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∧(¬Y∨Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218